   "num": "03",
   "kind": "short",
   "intro": "<p>Pulmonary fibrosis is a lung disease in which scar tissue forms in the walls of the alveoli.</p>",
   "parts": [
    {
     "type": "short",
     "id": "p-bl01a-03-1",
     "label": "03.1",
     "ref": "3.1.5.2",
     "stmts": [
      "3.1.5.2.s3",
      "3.1.5.2.s4"
     ],
     "ao": [
      "AO1"
     ],
     "marks": 3,
     "stem": "An oxygen molecule in inhaled air is in the trachea.<br>Name, in order, the airways it passes along to reach the gas-exchange surface, <strong>and</strong> describe how it then gets into a red blood cell in a capillary next to an alveolus.",
     "scheme": [
      {
       "point": "Trachea to bronchus (bronchi) to bronchioles to alveolus (alveoli), in this order.",
       "marks": 1
      },
      {
       "point": "(Diffuses) across the alveolar epithelium (wall of the alveolus).",
       "marks": 1
      },
      {
       "point": "(Then) across the capillary endothelium (capillary wall), into the plasma / red blood cell.",
       "marks": 1
      },
      {
       "point": "By diffusion, down a concentration gradient (from higher to lower partial pressure of oxygen).",
       "marks": 1
      }
     ],
     "alternatives": "Maximum 3 marks. Accept 'through the alveolar wall and the capillary wall' for the two layers. Ignore 'dissolves in the moisture lining the alveolus'. Reject 'osmosis' or 'active transport'. Reject an incorrect order of airways for the first point.",
     "model": "The oxygen molecule moves from the trachea into a bronchus, then along bronchioles to an alveolus. It then diffuses down its concentration gradient across the thin alveolar epithelium and the capillary endothelium into the plasma and so into a red blood cell.",
     "commonErrors": "Putting bronchioles before bronchi; naming only one of the two cell layers; saying oxygen is actively transported."
    },
    {
     "type": "short",
     "id": "p-bl01a-03-2",
     "label": "03.2",
     "ref": "3.1.5.2",
     "stmts": [
      "3.1.5.2.s7",
      "3.1.4.2.s3"
     ],
     "ao": [
      "AO2",
      "AO3"
     ],
     "marks": 2,
     "stem": "Doctors compared people with pulmonary fibrosis with healthy people of the same age. Table 3 shows some of their mean results.<figure><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;max-width:100%\"><table class=\"tbl\"><caption>Table 3</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\"><span class=\"sr-only\">Measurement</span></th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Healthy people</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">People with fibrosis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Thickness of the barrier between the air in the alveoli and the blood / µm</th><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">0.6</td><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">2.4</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Total surface area of alveoli available for gas exchange / m²</th><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">70</td><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">42</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit;padding:.3rem .4rem\">Partial pressure of oxygen in blood leaving the lungs, at rest / kPa</th><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">12.7</td><td style=\"padding:.3rem .4rem\">9.8</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figcaption class=\"small\">The values are illustrative.</figcaption></figure>Use Table 3 to explain why the partial pressure of oxygen in the blood leaving the lungs is lower in people with pulmonary fibrosis.",
     "scheme": [
      {
       "point": "A correct comparison from Table 3: the barrier is thicker (2.4 µm compared with 0.6 µm; four times thicker) or the surface area for gas exchange is smaller (42 m² compared with 70 m²).",
       "marks": 1
      },
      {
       "point": "Linked to diffusion: so the diffusion pathway (distance) is longer and/or the rate of diffusion of oxygen into the blood is lower (less oxygen diffuses into the blood per unit time).",
       "marks": 1
      }
     ],
     "alternatives": "The second mark requires the idea that diffusion is slower or takes place over a longer distance: two data comparisons with no reference to diffusion score 1 mark only. Data quotation is credited but not required. Accept 'slower diffusion'. Ignore 'less gas exchange' on its own for the second mark.",
     "model": "In fibrosis the barrier between alveolar air and blood is four times thicker (2.4 µm against 0.6 µm), so oxygen has a longer diffusion pathway, and the surface area available is smaller (42 m² against 70 m²). Both reduce the rate at which oxygen diffuses into the blood, so the blood leaving the lungs has a lower partial pressure of oxygen.",
     "commonErrors": "Saying the alveoli are 'damaged' without saying how this affects diffusion."
    }
   ]
