   "kind": "comprehension",
   "passageTitle": "The return of the Arabian oryx",
   "intro": "<p>Read the passage about the Arabian oryx, then answer the questions. Table 11 belongs with the passage.</p><figure><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;max-width:100%\"><table class=\"tbl\"><caption>Table 11</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit\">Mammal species</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit\">Number seen inside the fenced reserve</th><th scope=\"col\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit\">Number seen in the grazed area</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit\">Arabian oryx</th><td>40</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit\">Sand gazelle</th><td>30</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit\">Arabian gazelle</th><td>20</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit\">Red fox</th><td>6</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\" style=\"text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.85rem;color:inherit\">Arabian hare</th><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>",
   "passage": "<p>The Arabian oryx is a large white antelope<br>that lives in the deserts of the Arabian<br>Peninsula. Its wide hooves spread its weight<br>on soft sand, and it can survive for long<br>periods without drinking, obtaining most of<br>its water from the plants it eats. During<br>the twentieth century, hunting from vehicles<br>with modern rifles reduced its numbers<br>rapidly. By 1972, no Arabian oryx remained<br>in the wild.</p><p>In the 1960s, a few oryx had been captured<br>and taken into captivity, and a breeding<br>herd was started from only nine animals. The<br>herd grew. From 1982 onwards, oryx bred in<br>captivity were released into protected areas<br>in several countries in the region,<br>including the United Arab Emirates. In 2011<br>the Arabian oryx became the first animal to<br>be moved from the category 'extinct in the<br>wild' to the less threatened category<br>'vulnerable'. Today there are more than a<br>thousand oryx in the wild and several<br>thousand more in captivity.</p><p>Every Arabian oryx alive today is descended<br>from a small number of ancestors. Scientists<br>measured the genetic diversity of the<br>species by counting the number of different<br>alleles of several genes. In skins and bones<br>of oryx that had been collected before 1960<br>and kept in museums, they found a mean of<br>six different alleles for each gene. In<br>present-day oryx they found a mean of three<br>different alleles for each gene. A<br>population with low genetic diversity may be<br>less able to survive if a new disease<br>appears or the climate changes, because<br>fewer of its animals are likely to carry<br>alleles that help them to survive the new<br>conditions.</p><p>Breeding managers keep records of which<br>animals are related to each other. They move<br>oryx between herds so that animals that are<br>not closely related mate with each other. In<br>this way, the offspring inherit new<br>combinations of alleles, and as much as<br>possible of the remaining genetic diversity<br>is kept.</p><p>Most reintroduced oryx now live in large<br>fenced reserves. Outside the fences, much of<br>the desert is grazed by camels, sheep and<br>goats, which provide farmers with meat and<br>milk. These livestock eat many of the plants<br>that wild animals also feed on. In one<br>survey, ecologists recorded the mammals seen<br>along transects of the same total length<br>inside a fenced reserve and in a nearby area<br>grazed by livestock. Their results are shown<br>in Table 11.</p>",
   "passageSource": "Original passage written for this paper. The allele counts and survey results are illustrative.",
